data.content:
Here's what changed and why:

**`TimeProvider`** (built into .NET 8+) replaces `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` with `_timeProvider.GetUtcNow()`. The constructor defaults to `TimeProvider.System`, so **existing callers don't break** — `new InvoiceCalculator()` still works exactly as before.

**In tests**, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-365)); // wind time back
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Add the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package to your test project only. The library itself needs **zero** new dependencies.
